{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Set up {{ host.name }}: {{ product_name() }}{% endblock %}
{% block main %}
<p class="back"><a href="{{ url('manage_server') }}?host={{ host.name | urlencode }}">Back to {{ host.name }}</a></p>
<h1 class="page-title">What to set up on {{ host.name }}</h1>
<p>This makes a new account just for this tool, called {{ host.user }}. It must be a new one, not an account anyone logs in with: if it already exists, the commands stop without changing anything. Someone who can already be an administrator on the server pastes these there, all at once, from its provider's console or an SSH session.</p>
<p class="error">This account can act as an administrator: it has to, to read what only an administrator can, such as the login records. Whoever has this machine's key can run anything on the server as an administrator. If someone looks after security at your organization, they should know about it and agree.</p>
{% if not plain_account %}
<p class="error">These commands can't use the account name {{ host.user }}: it has to be a new, plainly named account, and not root. <a href="{{ url('manage_server') }}?host={{ host.name | urlencode }}">Choose a plain one</a>, such as scanner, or follow the guide in <code>docs/scanner-account.md</code>.</p>
{% elif not uses_sudo_rule %}
<p>This server is set up to {% if host.sudo == "root" %}log in as root{% else %}use sudo with a password{% endif %}, which doesn't use these commands. See "Other set-ups" in <code>docs/scanner-account.md</code>, or <a href="{{ url('manage_server') }}?host={{ host.name | urlencode }}">change it to sudo with no password</a>.</p>
{% elif not usable_python %}
<p class="error">These commands can't use the Python set for this server, {{ host.python }}: it has to be python3, python3.12 or a full path. <a href="{{ url('manage_server') }}?host={{ host.name | urlencode }}">Change it</a>.</p>
{% elif not public %}
<p class="error">This page needs the public part of the key this server logs in with, and it can't read one.{% if host.auth == "agent" %} This server logs in with an SSH agent, so its key isn't on this machine. Follow <code>docs/scanner-account.md</code> with your key.{% elif host.auth == "password" %} This server logs in with a password, which these commands don't set up. See <code>docs/scanner-account.md</code>.{% else %} <a href="{{ url('first_setup') }}">Make this machine's key</a>, or check the key file on <a href="{{ url('manage_server') }}?host={{ host.name | urlencode }}">its settings</a>.{% endif %}</p>
{% else %}
<h2>This machine's address</h2>
<p>With this, the key is refused from any other address, so it's far less use to anyone who copies it. Anything else sharing that address, such as other machines on the same internet connection, isn't kept out. Type the address your servers see this machine connecting from: on a home or office connection, a "what is my IP" site shows it from this machine. If it can change, leave it empty.</p>
{% if from_problem %}<p class="error" role="alert">{{ from_problem }}</p>{% endif %}
<form method="get" action="{{ url('manage_prepare') }}">
  <input type="hidden" name="host" value="{{ host.name }}">
  <label>Address <input name="from" value="{{ typed_from }}"></label>
  <button>Put it in the commands</button>
</form>
{% if not address %}<p class="note">These commands don't limit where the key can be used from.</p>{% endif %}
<h2>On the server</h2>
<pre class="command">{{ commands }}</pre>
<p>It should end by saying "Set up". If it says STOPPED, nothing was changed, and it says why. If it fails with "sudo: command not found" and you're logged in as root, remove the word <code>sudo</code> from the first line.</p>
{% if usual_python %}<p class="note">This expects Python at {{ python }}, as on RHEL 9 and 10, Rocky, AlmaLinux and Ubuntu 22.04 or later.</p>{% endif %}
<h2>Next</h2>
<p><a href="{{ url('manage_server') }}?host={{ host.name | urlencode }}">Confirm it's really this server</a>, then <a href="{{ url('check_new') }}?host={{ host.name | urlencode }}">run a check</a>.</p>
<details>
  <summary>Taking this away again</summary>
  <pre class="command">{{ removal }}</pre>
</details>
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
